{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "TCS Margin Staircase: Reading Profitability Layers",
  "scenario": "You're an analyst at a Mumbai equity desk reviewing TCS's five-year P&L data. Your desk lead asks you to compute all four margins (gross, EBITDA, EBIT, net) and diagnose where the company's profitability is strongest and where money is leaking out. Use the margin staircase to identify competitive position shifts.",
  "instructions": "Calculate gross, EBITDA, EBIT and net margins for each year; identify the widest and narrowest gaps between consecutive margins; diagnose which cost layer (operating, depreciation, or financing) poses the biggest profitability drag.",
  "learning_objective": "Calculate all four margin layers from a P&L; interpret gaps between margins to diagnose competitive strength, operating efficiency and financing burden; spot margin trends that reveal competitive moat shifts.",
  "hints": [
    "Start by computing gross margin as (Gross Profit ÷ Revenue) × 100 for the first year — it's the cleanest read of pricing power.",
    "For the gap analysis, subtract each lower margin from the upper one (e.g. EBITDA margin − gross margin) to isolate the cost of operations; the largest gap reveals where money is leaking fastest.",
    "Use INDEX-MATCH to find the year with the widest gap between EBIT and net margin — that year's high financing or tax drag explains the net margin weakness most directly."
  ],
